sketch-orca oracle patch 0007: every step boundary in full, under SKETCH_ORCA_ORACLE_DUMP.
Modified for Sketch, 2026-11-13.

With SKETCH_ORCA_ORACLE_DUMP=<folder> (patch 0005), each dump holds every value the step after it
reads, so that the Rust translation's Print::process() steps can be checked one boundary at a time
(parity/SOP1.md):
- object<i>-<step>-<n>.sop1 adds, after its OBJ, LAYR and SUPL records, an OSTA record (the shared
  object, the slicing parameters, the instances, the first layer wall extruders, the brim box, the
  object's own skirt and the first layer slices by volume and group), an OWRN record (the object's
  step states and warnings) and a LEXT record per layer (the layers below and above, the lslices'
  boxes, the overhangs' box and the curled lines);
- front-config-<n>.sop1 adds a CONF "default-object" and a CONF "default-region", an FPRT record
  (the plate, the origin, the filament maps, the print regions and the skirt, brim and wipe tower
  flags) and an FOBJ record per print object (its size, transformation, instances, slicing
  parameters, volumes and region layout);
- print-<step>-<n>.sop1 adds, after CNFL, a PWRN record (the print's step states and warnings), a
  PXTR record (the conflict's instances, the objects' first layer wall extruders, brim boxes and
  skirts, the unprintable filaments, the filament maps, the filament change statistics and the fake
  wipe tower) and a TLXT record (the tool ordering's custom G-code);
- Print::process() writes print-psConflictCheck-<n>.sop1 at its end, after the conflict check and
  before "Slicing process finished.": the end of process().
OracleDump.cpp keeps its lines 1-1017 where patch 0006 left them, as the Rust translation follows
them line by line. Five of them change in place (18, 66, 770, 783 and 1013) and every new record is
written after line 1017. It reads no knob of its own. A dump folder that does not exist stops the
oracle before it does anything else, with exit code 78 (patch 0005). Unset, nothing is written and
nothing changes.

